.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt I915 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm i915
.Nd Intel Haswell graphics driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd rach2.i915
.Cd rach2.i915.firstlight
.Cd rach2.i915fake
.Pp
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/i915_drm.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/renderD Ns Ar M
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN/gt_*_freq_mhz
.Pp
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN/power/rc6_*
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver binds the graphics device of an Intel Haswell processor with GT2
graphics, the HD 4400 of the Lenovo G50-70 (PCI 8086:0a16) among them:
the twenty device ids that Linux 6.12 gives the same device data.
It binds function 0 at 00:02.0 only, as Linux does.
.Pp
The driver binds a device only when the kernel command line has
.Cm rach2.i915 .
Without it the kernel logs the device and leaves the screen to the
firmware's framebuffer.
.Pp
With
.Cm rach2.i915fake
the driver also binds, for tests in QEMU, each function of QEMU's
.Ql edu
device (PCI 1234:11e8) with a fake chip behind it in place of the
hardware: the fake answers as the Lenovo's HD 4400, so the function's
.Pa /sys
entries report 8086:0a16, revision 0b, board 17aa:380c and class
0x030000, and libdrm and Mesa find a Haswell GT2; its rings complete each
command as it is handed over, without reading batches; its message is a
real MSI of the edu function; it has no stolen memory; and its aperture is
memory the fake fills from each page the GTT maps there and writes back
when it syncs, detiled through the fence registers as the chip would.
Its file
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug/dri/DEVICE/i915_fake
injects faults, as
.Xr debugfs 5
describes.
.Pp
A bound device is a DRM device, as
.Xr drm 4
describes, registered as
.Ql i915
with Linux's identity: description
.Ql Intel Graphics ,
version 1.6.0 and date 20230929, on the PCI bus.
It has a card node and a render node, and the features
.Dv DRIVER_GEM ,
.Dv DRIVER_RENDER ,
.Dv DRIVER_SYNCOBJ
and
.Dv DRIVER_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE .
It does not yet set modes, so it has neither
.Dv DRIVER_MODESET
nor
.Dv DRIVER_ATOMIC ,
as Linux's i915 on a device without display.
.Pp
At bind the driver maps the registers, the first 2 MiB of BAR 0,
uncached; turns on memory decoding and bus mastering; masks, disables and
clears every interrupt source of the display engine, the GT, power
management and the south display; then takes a vector and enables the
device's MSI message on it, and turns the display engine's master enable
on with every source still masked.
A machine without message vectors leaves the device without interrupts;
the driver then polls for its work.
.Pp
It reads the firmware's description of the outputs and the panel: the
ACPI OpRegion at the address in the ASLS register, and the video BIOS
table it holds, or else the one in the option ROM's shadow at 0xc0000.
The kernel logs
.Dq i915: opregion
with its version and mailboxes, and
.Dq i915: vbt
with the table's signature, source, version and number of outputs, or that
there is none and each port takes Linux's defaults.
.Pp
It then starts the render and blitter rings: for each a status page and a
ring of 16 KiB in the global GTT, the ring stopped, emptied and started
again with every wait bounded.
Each ring's user interrupt is enabled and stays masked except while the
driver waits for that ring's work; every other source stays masked.
A ring that does not start is logged, and the device takes no work.
.Pp
With
.Cm rach2.i915.firstlight
the render ring writes one value to a page of the global GTT before any
display code runs, and the kernel logs
.Dq i915: first light: ok
or the failure by name: no room in the ring, the submission refused, no
breadcrumb, the value read back, or no user interrupt.
.Pp
The GT sleeps in RC6 while it is idle, with Linux's thresholds: under the
hardware's timers after 64 ms without work, and at once while the driver
holds it parked.
Its frequency moves in steps of 50 MHz between RPn and RP0, the chip's
limits, which the bind reads with the efficient frequency RPe from the
chip's power controller.
While the GT has work the chip's up and down interrupts move the
frequency, in steps that double while they go one way: up when the GT was
busy for more than 95% of an interval of 16 ms at low frequencies, 13 ms
between and 10 ms near RP0; down when it was busy for less than 85% of
32 ms; and on the down timeout, to RPe and then RPn.
Parked, the GT runs at RPn.
The bind leaves the GT parked, and logs
.Dq i915: rps:
with RPn, RP1, RP0, RPe and the highest frequency allowed.
.Pp
The kernel logs
.Dq i915: found
with the device's place, id, subplatform and revision, and
.Dq i915: registered
with its card.
.Sh IOCTLS
The core's ioctls are those of
.Xr drm 4 .
The driver's own,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_*
from
.Dv DRM_COMMAND_BASE ,
fall in the families below.
.Ss Identity and parameters
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_VERSION
answers as above.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GETPARAM
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_SETPARAM
are not yet served and fail with
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Buffers
Buffer objects follow Linux's i915 on an LLC part.
Their pages are anonymous memory, written back, and are bound into the
global GTT when the aperture or the GPU needs them.
These calls are served on the card node and the render node alike:
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_CACHING
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CREATE
An object of
.Fa size
bytes rounded up to a page, and its handle.
.Er EINVAL
for 0 and
.Er E2BIG
past what an object holds.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_PREAD , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_PWRITE
Copy bytes out of the object or into it, after its work is done.
PREAD reads through the processor's view; PWRITE writes through the
aperture when the object's caching would need the processor's lines
flushed, and otherwise through the processor's view.
An object imported from another device has no pages of its own and is
read and written through the aperture.
.Er EINVAL
past the object's end,
.Er EFAULT
for a buffer outside the caller's memory.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_DOMAIN , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SW_FINISH
Move the object into the CPU, GTT or WC domain before the caller touches a
map of it, flushing and invalidating what the move needs; SW_FINISH
flushes the processor's writes of an object that is scanned out.
.Er EINVAL
for a GPU domain or a write domain other than the read domains.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MMAP_OFFSET
The offset to give
.Xr mmap 2
on the node for a map of the kind
.Fa flags
names:
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_GTT
through the aperture, detiled by a fence;
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_WB
the object's pages written back;
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_WC
and
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_UC
write-combining and uncached.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_MMAP_GTT
shares the call's number and gives the GTT kind.
.Er ENODEV
for WC and UC without PAT, for
.Dv I915_MMAP_OFFSET_FIXED ,
and for a map of an import's pages;
.Er EINVAL
for an extension, since none is defined.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_TILING , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_TILING
Set or read the object's layout, linear, X or Y, its stride, and the
swizzle of bit 6 programs must apply to address a tiled object's pages:
on Haswell none, or 9 and 10 for X tiles and 9 for Y when the memory
controller interleaves its two channels.
A stride must be a multiple of the tile's width, 512 bytes for X and 128
for Y, and at most 256 KiB.
GTT maps of the object fault again after a change.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_SET_CACHING , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_GET_CACHING
Set or read the object's level in the processor's caches:
.Dv I915_CACHING_NONE ,
.Dv I915_CACHING_CACHED ,
or
.Dv I915_CACHING_DISPLAY ,
which Haswell's GT2 serves uncached.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_BUSY , DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_WAIT
Whether engines still read or write the object, and a wait for them of
.Fa timeout_ns ,
for ever when it is negative, with the time left written back;
.Er ETIME
when the object is still busy at the timeout.
.El
.Pp
A fault on a GTT map binds the object inside the aperture whole when it
fits, or else a view of at least 1 MiB around the page in whole tile
rows, and gives it one of the 32 fence registers, the least recently used
that no fault holds.
A map whose binding or fence is taken away faults again on its next
touch; one that finds no room or no fence raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
.Pp
The core's PRIME calls export an object as a dma-buf, which
.Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC
moves into the CPU domain and back to the GTT's, and import another
device's dma-buf as an object.
All of these calls fail with
.Er ENOENT
for a handle that names no object.
.Ss Contexts
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_CONTEXT_CREATE
and the context calls are not yet served and fail with
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Submission
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_I915_GEM_EXECBUFFER2
and its variants are not yet served and fail with
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Display
Mode setting is not yet served; see above.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/dri/renderDM
.It Pa /dev/dri/cardN
The card node of a bound device.
.It Pa /dev/dri/renderD Ns Ar M
Its render node.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/debug/dri/DEVICE/i915_fake
The fault file of a device bound to the fake chip.
.El
.Ss The card in /sys
The card's directory,
.Pa /sys/class/drm/cardN ,
holds Linux's files of the GT's frequency, each a decimal number of MHz
and a newline:
.Bl -tag -width gt_boost_freq_mhz
.It Pa gt_act_freq_mhz
The frequency the GT runs at, as the chip reports it.
.It Pa gt_cur_freq_mhz
The frequency the driver last asked for.
.It Pa gt_boost_freq_mhz
The frequency a boost would ask for; at bind the highest frequency
allowed: RP0, or the overclocking limit of a chip that has one.
.It Pa gt_max_freq_mhz
The highest frequency the driver asks for; RP0 at bind, and up to the
overclocking limit of a chip that has one.
.It Pa gt_min_freq_mhz
The lowest frequency the driver asks for; RPn at bind.
.It Pa gt_RP0_freq_mhz , gt_RP1_freq_mhz , gt_RPn_freq_mhz
The chip's highest frequency, its guaranteed one, and its lowest.
.El
.Pp
The boost, max and min files are written by root with a number in
decimal, in hexadecimal after
.Ql 0x
or in octal after
.Ql 0 ,
and at most one newline, as Linux's
.Fn kstrtou32
reads it; the number is rounded to the nearest 50 MHz.
A write fails with
.Er EINVAL
when it is no number, when it lies outside RPn and the highest frequency
allowed, or when it would put max below min or min above max, and with
.Er ERANGE
when it does not fit in 32 bits.
A new max or min moves the frequency into the new limits at once.
.Pp
Below it,
.Pa power
holds RC6's files:
.Bl -tag -width rc6_residency_ms
.It Pa power/rc6_enable
The RC6 states enabled, in hexadecimal: 1 for RC6, 0 without it.
.It Pa power/rc6_residency_ms
The milliseconds the GT has spent in RC6 since the bind, in decimal,
wrapping at 2^32.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr backlight 4 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr watchdog 4 ,
.Xr debugfs 5
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux 6.12's i915, whose register names and device
table it takes.
